pr-checkout [PR-NUMBER]

Pick one of the current repo's OPEN pull requests via fzf and switch
to its branch in place.

Only open PRs are listed -- merged and closed ones are noise when the
intent is "go work on this". Draft PRs count as open and are shown
with a "draft" state. Rows you authored are colored green, matching
the convention in pr-worktree's picker.

Pass a PR number to skip the picker entirely.

Picker columns:

  #NUM  STATE  CHECKS  REVIEW  AUTHOR  TITLE  (wt: <dir>)

  STATE   open or draft.
  CHECKS  check rollup: "ok N", "fail N/M", "pend N/M", or "-".
          SKIPPED and NEUTRAL contexts are excluded from the
          denominator.
  REVIEW  approved / changes-req / review-req, or "-".
  (wt:)   present when the PR's branch is already checked out in
          another worktree of this repo -- see below.

The preview pane shows `gh pr view` for the highlighted PR.

WORKTREES. A branch can only be checked out in one worktree at a
time, so `git switch` to a branch that another worktree holds fails
outright ("fatal: ... is already used by worktree at ..."). Those
PRs are still listed, annotated `(wt: <dirname>)`, and selecting one
cds you into that existing worktree instead of failing. Either way
you end up looking at the branch.

The worktree you are currently standing in is never annotated --
switching to your own current branch is a no-op, not a conflict.

Branch resolution: most open PRs have no local branch yet, so this
defers to `gh pr checkout`, which creates the branch, sets up
tracking, and handles PRs from forks. When a local branch already
exists it is a plain `git switch`.

Related:

  switchbranch   local branches only, no PR context.
  pr-worktree    picks a PR but creates a NEW worktree for it.
  pr-list        lists PRs across a multi-repo collection dir.

Examples:

  pr-checkout
  pr-checkout 3726

Requires: gh, git, jq, fzf.
